title: The Trial in Moscow
subtitle: Twelve Socialist Revolutionaries are sentenced to death.
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1922 and month = 8 and e22_three_int = 1
max-visits: 1

= The Trial in Moscow

On 7 August the court in the Hall of Columns sentences twelve leaders of the Socialist Revolutionaries to death. In April Radek gave the social democrats our word that it would not come to this, and German workers marched on that word. *Vorwärts* prints the guarantee on its front page beside the verdict.

- @protest: Protest to Moscow in public.
- @defend: Defend the verdict.

@protest
subtitle: The party keeps its word. Zinoviev will not forget it.
on-arrival: {!
RG.ecci('zinoviev', -8);
RG.ecci('lenin', -3);
Q.independence += 7;
Q.spd_relation += 4;
Q.lean_luxemburgist += 2;
!}

[? if luxemburg_alive = 1 : Rosa Luxemburg's open letter quotes what she wrote in prison in 1918: freedom only for the supporters of the government is no freedom at all. ?][? if luxemburg_alive = 0 : Clara Zetkin writes to Lenin, and the Zentrale asks in public for the promise to be kept. ?] The sentences are suspended a few days later. Moscow says this was always the plan.

@defend
subtitle: Loyal to Moscow. Every SPD speaker in Germany now has a quotation ready.
on-arrival: {!
RG.ecci('zinoviev', 5);
Q.independence -= 4;
Q.spd_relation -= 12;
!}

*Die Rote Fahne* explains the case against the defendants over two pages. In the works our members are asked what a communist promise is worth, and have no good answer.
